CAT = "caguardLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
# Syllabus sections 7 and 8: the Canadian legal system, evidence, and legal authorities
# (Criminal Code, Trespass to Property Act, other Ontario statutes, PIPEDA, tort law).
Q = [
("Which level of government makes Canada's criminal law?",
 "Federal Parliament", ["The provincial legislature", "The municipal council", "The police services board"],
 "Criminal law is federal and set out mainly in the Criminal Code. Provinces make provincial offences and municipalities make bylaws."),
("The Trespass to Property Act is a law of which level of government?",
 "Provincial", ["Federal", "Municipal", "Federal and municipal"],
 "The Trespass to Property Act is an Ontario statute, and charges under it are provincial offences. It is not part of the Criminal Code."),
("Who makes bylaws such as noise and parking rules?",
 "Municipalities", ["The federal government", "The province only", "Property owners"],
 "Bylaws are made by municipal councils and vary from one municipality to another. Property owners can set site rules, but those are not bylaws."),
("What is a summary conviction offence?",
 "A less serious criminal offence", ["A provincial offence", "The most serious kind of crime", "A civil wrong"],
 "Summary conviction offences are the less serious Criminal Code offences, with lower maximum penalties. Indictable offences are the serious ones."),
("For citizen's arrest purposes, a hybrid offence is treated as:",
 "Indictable", ["Summary", "Provincial", "Civil"],
 "A hybrid offence, which the Crown may prosecute either way, is deemed indictable until the Crown elects. That is why it can support a citizen's arrest for an indictable offence."),
("Under the Criminal Code, anyone may arrest without warrant a person they find committing:",
 "An indictable offence", ["Any bylaw offence", "Any provincial offence", "Any traffic offence"],
 "Section 494(1) lets anyone arrest a person found committing an indictable offence. Bylaw and provincial offences do not fall under it."),
("As the owner's authorized agent, you may arrest someone you find committing what on or in relation to the property?",
 "Any criminal offence", ["Only an indictable offence", "Only a violent offence", "A provincial parking offence"],
 "Section 494(2) lets the owner or a person authorized by the owner arrest someone found committing any criminal offence on or in relation to that property. The indictable-only limit applies to arrests elsewhere."),
("Since 2013, an owner's agent may also arrest within a reasonable time after a property offence if they reasonably believe:",
 "Police cannot feasibly do it", ["The suspect has left the area", "The suspect is under 18", "A manager has approved it"],
 "The Citizen's Arrest and Self-defence Act added a delayed arrest power: within a reasonable time, where it is not feasible in the circumstances for a peace officer to make the arrest."),
("After making a citizen's arrest, what must you do?",
 "Hand them to police forthwith", ["Release them after about an hour", "Hold them for the manager", "Let them go with a warning"],
 "Section 494(3) requires anyone making a citizen's arrest to deliver the person to a peace officer forthwith. Holding someone longer than needed invites liability."),
("What does finding someone committing an offence require?",
 "You saw the offence yourself", ["A witness told you about it", "CCTV footage showed it the next day", "You strongly suspect it"],
 "Finds committing means you witnessed the offence as it happened. Second-hand reports and later footage are matters for police."),
("Section 494(1)(b) lets anyone arrest a person they believe on reasonable grounds committed a criminal offence and who is:",
 "Fleeing and freshly pursued", ["Known to have a record", "Refusing to show ID", "Loitering near the site"],
 "Anyone may arrest a person escaping from and freshly pursued by people with lawful authority to arrest them. A record or refusal to show ID is not grounds."),
("Under the Trespass to Property Act, how can notice prohibiting entry be given?",
 "Orally, in writing or by signs", ["Only in writing, by registered mail", "Only by posted signs", "Only through police"],
 "Notice can be spoken, written, or given by signs or markings. A clear spoken direction is enough."),
("Under the Trespass to Property Act, what do red markings on a property mean?",
 "Entry is prohibited", ["Some activities are banned", "Hunting is allowed", "Parking only"],
 "Red markings give notice that entry is prohibited. Yellow markings mean certain activities are prohibited."),
("Under the Trespass to Property Act, what do yellow markings mean?",
 "Some activities are prohibited", ["Entry is prohibited", "The area is under video watch", "Visitors must sign in"],
 "Yellow markings give notice that certain activities are prohibited, which should be stated on the notice. Red means no entry at all."),
("Under the Trespass to Property Act, when may you arrest a trespasser?",
 "While on the premises unlawfully", ["Only once they have left the site", "Only if they damage property", "Only when police are present"],
 "The occupier or an authorized person may arrest without warrant someone believed on reasonable grounds to be on the premises in breach of the Act. Once they have left, the power is gone."),
("After a trespass arrest, what must you do?",
 "Call police promptly", ["Release them at the gate", "Charge them yourself", "Hold them until morning"],
 "The Act requires the person arrested to be handed to a police officer promptly. Guards cannot lay or process the charge on their own."),
("Under the Trespass to Property Act, a person told to leave commits an offence if they:",
 "Do not leave immediately", ["Do not leave within 24 hours", "Argue with the guard", "Leave by a side exit"],
 "Not leaving immediately after being directed to by the occupier or an authorized person is an offence. Arguing is not, as long as they go."),
("When enforcing site trespass rules, a guard acts as:",
 "The occupier's agent", ["A peace officer", "A bylaw officer", "An officer of the court"],
 "A guard's trespass authority comes from acting on behalf of the owner or occupier. Guards are not peace or bylaw officers."),
("On the public sidewalk outside your site, your trespass authority is:",
 "None past the property line", ["The same as on the site itself", "Wider than on the site", "Shared with the city"],
 "You act for the occupier of your site, so that authority ends at the property line. Public property belongs to the municipality."),
("Taking property with intent to deprive the owner of it is:",
 "Theft", ["Mischief", "Robbery", "Fraud"],
 "Theft is taking or converting property with intent to deprive the owner. Robbery is theft involving violence or threats."),
("Robbery is best described as:",
 "Theft with violence or threats", ["Theft from a parked car", "Entering a building at night", "Keeping a lost wallet"],
 "Robbery combines theft with violence or threats of violence, which makes it far more serious. Entering a place unlawfully is break and enter."),
("Spray-painting graffiti on a wall is most likely:",
 "Mischief", ["Theft", "Robbery", "Fraud"],
 "Mischief is wilfully destroying or damaging property, or interfering with its lawful use. Nothing is taken, so it is not theft."),
("Breaking into a closed store to steal is:",
 "Break and enter", ["Mischief only", "Trespass at night", "Possession only"],
 "Breaking and entering a place with intent to commit an indictable offence, or committing one inside, is break and enter. Trespass at night is a separate offence near a dwelling."),
("Which act can be an assault even if no one is touched?",
 "A threat by act or gesture", ["Refusing to give your name", "Walking away from a guard", "Swearing at a closed door"],
 "Assault includes attempting or threatening by act or gesture to apply force, where the person can carry it out. Swearing alone with no threat is not assault."),
("Shouting and swearing in a public place so as to disturb others can be:",
 "Causing a disturbance", ["Criminal harassment", "Mischief", "Theft of service"],
 "Causing a disturbance covers fighting, shouting, swearing or being drunk in a public place in a way that disturbs others. Harassment requires repeated conduct causing fear."),
("Repeatedly following someone so they fear for their safety is:",
 "Criminal harassment", ["Causing a disturbance", "Mischief", "Trespass at night"],
 "Criminal harassment is repeated following, communicating or watching that causes a person to reasonably fear for their safety. It is often called stalking."),
("Knowingly keeping a phone that you know was stolen is:",
 "Possession of stolen property", ["Theft under $5,000", "Mischief to data", "Breach of trust"],
 "Having property you know was obtained by crime is possession of stolen property, even if you did not steal it yourself."),
("What is continuity of evidence?",
 "An unbroken record of who held it", ["Keeping evidence in one place", "Collecting all the evidence each shift", "Photographing evidence only"],
 "Continuity, or chain of custody, documents every person who handled evidence from collection to court. A gap can make the evidence unusable."),
("What should you mark on an evidence bag you seal?",
 "Your initials, date and time", ["The suspect's address", "The client's logo", "Nothing, to avoid damage"],
 "Mark the bag with your initials and the date and time so the item can be identified and its continuity proven."),
("Why limit how many people handle a piece of evidence?",
 "To protect its continuity", ["To keep it clean for resale", "To save on evidence bags", "To keep it from the police"],
 "Every extra handler is another link to document and another chance of contamination. Fewer handlers make the chain of custody easier to prove."),
("Which federal law governs personal information collected by private businesses?",
 "PIPEDA", ["The Privacy Act", "FIPPA", "The PSISA"],
 "The Personal Information Protection and Electronic Documents Act covers private-sector organizations. The federal Privacy Act and Ontario's FIPPA cover government bodies."),
("CCTV footage of a customer may be shared:",
 "Only as policy and law allow", ["With anyone who asks", "On social media to identify them", "With friends who know them"],
 "Personal information, including video, must be used and disclosed only for proper purposes under privacy law and site policy. Posting it publicly can breach privacy law."),
("In a licensed bar, what must staff do for a patron who appears intoxicated?",
 "Not serve them more alcohol", ["Serve only beer from now on", "Serve them with food only", "Ask a friend to order for them"],
 "Ontario's liquor law prohibits serving alcohol to anyone who appears intoxicated, and licensees must not permit drunkenness on the premises. Ordering through a friend is still serving them."),
("What is the legal drinking age in Ontario?",
 "19", ["18", "20", "21"],
 "The legal age to buy and drink alcohol in Ontario is 19. Some provinces use 18, which is why it is a common mistake."),
("Under Ontario's Residential Tenancies Act, how much written notice must a landlord normally give before entering a unit?",
 "24 hours", ["1 hour", "48 hours", "7 days"],
 "Except in emergencies or when the tenant consents, a landlord must give 24 hours' written notice and enter between 8 a.m. and 8 p.m. Guards at residential sites must respect that."),
("During a lawful strike at your site, your role is to:",
 "Stay neutral and protect the site", ["Remove picketers from the public street", "Join the picket line", "Take sides with management"],
 "Guards protect people and property and must stay neutral in a labour dispute. Picketing on public property is a police matter, not yours."),
("After how many hours a week does overtime pay normally start under Ontario's Employment Standards Act?",
 "44", ["40", "48", "50"],
 "Ontario's ESA sets overtime at one and a half times the regular rate after 44 hours in a work week. Many US states use 40."),
("Trespass to Property Act charges are dealt with under:",
 "The Provincial Offences Act", ["The Criminal Code", "The Youth Criminal Justice Act", "The PSISA"],
 "Provincial offences such as trespass and many liquor offences are prosecuted under the Provincial Offences Act. Criminal Code offences are federal."),
("What is a tort?",
 "A civil wrong", ["A minor crime", "A provincial offence", "A type of bylaw"],
 "A tort is a civil wrong, such as negligence or false imprisonment, for which the injured person can sue for damages. It is separate from any criminal charge."),
("Detaining a shopper you wrongly suspect, without legal grounds, can lead to a lawsuit for:",
 "False imprisonment", ["Nuisance", "Trespass to land", "Breach of contract"],
 "Holding someone without lawful authority is the tort of false imprisonment, even without force. It is the most common lawsuit against loss prevention staff."),
("Loudly and falsely calling a customer a thief in front of others risks:",
 "Defamation", ["Mischief", "Nuisance", "Negligence"],
 "A false statement that harms a person's reputation, made to others, can be defamation. Keep accusations private and factual."),
("A guard ignores a known icy entrance and a visitor falls. Which tort is this?",
 "Negligence", ["Assault", "Defamation", "Trespass"],
 "Negligence is failing to take the care a reasonable person would, causing foreseeable harm. Reporting and managing known hazards is part of duty of care."),
("Why can a security company be sued for a guard's wrongful act on duty?",
 "Vicarious liability", ["Strict criminal liability", "Joint tenancy", "Absolute privilege"],
 "Under vicarious liability, an employer can be held responsible for torts its employees commit in the course of their work. The guard can be sued personally as well."),
("What should you promptly tell a person you arrest?",
 "The reason for the arrest", ["Your home address", "That they will be charged", "Your employer's phone number"],
 "Section 29 of the Criminal Code requires anyone making an arrest to tell the person the reason, where it is feasible to do so. Give the same reason you will give police. Whether charges are laid is for police and the Crown."),
("Which right should an arrested person be told about without delay?",
 "The right to a lawyer", ["The right to bail now", "The right to a jury", "The right to leave"],
 "Section 10(b) of the Charter gives an arrested person the right to a lawyer without delay. Courts have generally held the Charter does not bind private guards directly, but guard training teaches telling the person of this right because it protects them and the case. Bail is decided later by police or a court."),
("What does reasonable grounds mean?",
 "Facts a reasonable person accepts", ["A strong hunch from your own experience", "Proof beyond any doubt", "An order from a manager"],
 "Reasonable grounds are facts that would lead an ordinary, prudent person to the same belief. A hunch is not enough, and proof beyond doubt is the trial standard."),
("What is the standard of proof in a criminal trial?",
 "Beyond a reasonable doubt", ["Balance of probabilities", "Reasonable suspicion", "Clear and present danger"],
 "The Crown must prove guilt beyond a reasonable doubt. Civil cases, such as a lawsuit against a guard, use the lower balance of probabilities."),
("What is the standard of proof in a civil lawsuit?",
 "Balance of probabilities", ["Beyond a reasonable doubt", "Reasonable suspicion", "Proof beyond any doubt"],
 "In a civil case the plaintiff must show their version is more likely than not. That is why someone can be acquitted criminally and still lose a lawsuit."),
("Which of these is a peace officer under the Criminal Code?",
 "A police officer", ["A licensed security guard", "A loss prevention agent", "A private investigator"],
 "Police officers are peace officers; licensed guards and investigators are not, whatever their duties. Guards may not hold themselves out as police."),
("Which law deals with young people aged 12 to 17 accused of crimes?",
 "The Youth Criminal Justice Act", ["The Child, Youth and Family Act", "The Provincial Offences Act", "The Education Act"],
 "The federal Youth Criminal Justice Act governs how youths aged 12 to 17 are dealt with for criminal offences. The arrest powers are the same; the process after is different."),
("Under the Criminal Code, anyone who witnesses a breach of the peace may:",
 "Interfere to stop it continuing", ["Arrest anyone who is standing nearby", "Fine those involved", "Search everyone involved"],
 "Section 30 justifies interfering to prevent a breach of the peace continuing and detaining a person to give them to a peace officer, using no more force than reasonably necessary."),
("Past court decisions that guide later cases are called:",
 "Case law", ["Statutes", "Bylaws", "Regulations"],
 "Case law, or precedent, is the body of court decisions courts follow in similar cases. Statutes and regulations are written by legislatures and governments."),
]
